# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
BLOCKS = {
    "base": [
        ("One grade, two base materials under a {city} ribbon",
         "<p>Pavers want a dense-graded aggregate compacted tight enough to carry a tire's point load, while the turf beside them needs the opposite: a washed, open-graded crushed rock or crushed concrete layer that lets water pass straight through instead of locking together. On a {city} paver-and-turf job the two materials meet at a single finished grade set before either one goes down, so the strip doesn't sit proud of or sunk below the hardscape once both are compacted. Keeping the layout continuous while letting the base underneath change at the joint is what makes the two materials behave as one surface instead of two competing ones.</p>"),
        ("How deep the washed rock runs under a narrow strip",
         "<p>A turf strip tucked between pavers still gets the standard two to four inches of washed, open-graded crushed rock or crushed concrete a full lawn would get, even where the ribbon itself is only a handful of inches wide. Skimping on depth because the footprint is small is a common shortcut, and it backfires faster in a confined joint than it would in an open yard, since a narrow strip has nowhere to shed excess water sideways once the base underneath is too shallow to clear it. On a {city} driveway ribbon, that depth is what keeps a hose rinse draining in seconds instead of pooling along the paver edge.</p>"),
        ("Why unwashed fill causes more trouble in a paver joint",
         "<p>Florida's turf standard requires subgrade material to be washed before it goes in the ground, specifically so loose fines don't bind together into a crust that blocks water from passing through. That rule matters more in a paver-and-turf joint than in an open lawn, because a narrow strip boxed in by two hard edges has less surface area to compensate if the base underneath starts holding water instead of moving it. Unwashed fill under a {city} stepping-stone path shows up first as a soft, spongy tread rather than a puddle anyone notices right away, since the paver on either side hides it from view.</p>"),
        ("Crowning a ribbon so water doesn't sit against the paver",
         "<p>A plain lawn's base slopes one to two percent away from the house and calls it done, but a driveway ribbon boxed in by paver courses on both sides needs its own slight crown down the middle instead, since there's no open ground nearby for water to run off toward. We set that crown into the base before the turf goes on, aimed so runoff sheds toward the paver joints rather than pooling in the center of a {city} ribbon after a storm. The grade percentage doesn't change from what any other turf project uses, only the direction the crown points and the reason we chose it.</p>"),
        ("Why we don't over-compact the sand under a paver-and-turf layout",
         "<p>The state's subgrade rule sets a limit as well as a minimum: native soil under any turf, including the strip beside a {city} paver patio, can't be packed so hard that it stops water from percolating down through it. That limit is easy to miss on a paver-and-turf job specifically, because the paver zone right next to the turf strip does get compacted tight for bearing capacity, and it's tempting to treat the whole footprint the same way for consistency. Keeping the turf side of the joint compacted firm but not sealed is what lets the native sand underneath keep doing its share of the drainage work.</p>"),
        ("Why the joint gets its own edge restraint, not just two base types",
         "<p>Beyond the two different base materials meeting at one grade, a {city} paver-and-turf joint usually gets a physical edge restraint too: a bender board, a metal edging strip, or the paver's own edge course set in its bedding, right at the line where the dense-graded aggregate under the pavers meets the washed rock under the turf. Without that restraint, the two materials can creep into each other under years of foot traffic and rain, blurring a joint that was cut crisp on installation day. Setting the restraint before either base finishes compacting keeps that boundary fixed rather than gradually drifting.</p>"),
        ("Measuring a pond setback from the water, not from the pavers",
         "<p>A {city} paver-and-turf patio near a pond or canal has to keep its turf sections at least ten feet from the water's edge unless a seawall separates the two, and that distance is measured from the actual waterline, not from wherever the paver hardscape happens to end. A layout where pavers run closer to the water than the turf does is fine, since the setback applies specifically to the turf, but a design that assumes the whole patio can hug the water evenly usually needs its turf squares pulled back farther than the paver squares beside them.</p>"),
        ("Keeping a septic lid reachable under a paver-and-turf walkway",
         "<p>A stepping-stone path or a paver-bordered turf walkway crossing toward a septic drainfield on a {city} property still has to leave the tank's lid reachable for pump-out, which means neither a fixed paver nor a glued turf section gets set permanently over it. Planning the path's layout around that lid before bedding sand or base rock goes down avoids discovering the conflict after the walkway is finished, when moving a paver course or cutting back a turf edge is a much bigger job than adjusting a chalk line would have been.</p>"),
    ],
    "product": [
        ("Why a driveway ribbon needs a shorter, denser blade",
         "<p>A family lawn can carry a taller, looser pile and still look full, but a ribbon down the center of a {city} driveway calls for a shorter, denser blade instead, since a taller pile tends to lean toward whichever side gets more shade or gets brushed less often. A dense, upright blade in the three-quarter to one-inch range holds its shape in a strip only a few inches wide, where there's no surrounding grass to visually soften a lean or a mat. The same reasoning applies to a stepping-stone path, where the turf between treads gets stepped on directly rather than walked around.</p>"),
        ("Keeping infill inside a joint only a few inches wide",
         "<p>Infill choice changes when the turf sits in a joint instead of an open lawn, because a strip a few inches across gives loose material less room to settle before it reaches the paver edge and works its way into the joint sand next door. Clean silica sand or a coated sand, brushed in at the usual one to two pounds per square foot, still meets the state's natural-material rule for a residential lot, but we favor the coated version on a {city} ribbon since it resists shifting sideways under foot and tire traffic better than plain sand does in that narrow a space.</p>"),
        ("Matching backing drainage to a strip boxed in by pavers",
         "<p>An open lawn's backing has an entire yard to spread water across if one section runs slow, but a {city} turf strip pinned between two paver courses doesn't have that margin, so the backing's drainage rating matters more here than the square footage would suggest. A product rated to pass more than thirty inches of water an hour clears a joint fast enough that a hose rinse or a summer downpour doesn't linger against either paver edge. Choosing a lower-rated backing to save cost on a small strip is one of the few places where the square footage saved doesn't offset the drainage lost.</p>"),
        ("What a stepping-stone path asks of the turf between treads",
         "<p>Turf cut for a stepping-stone path takes a different kind of wear than a driveway ribbon, since every square inch between the treads gets stepped on directly rather than driven over occasionally. A denser, shorter pile resists the repeated point-load of a foot landing in the same four to eight inches of gap between pavers on a {city} path, where a taller residential blend would compress and stay flattened along the exact line people actually walk. The cut pieces themselves are usually small and irregular, following the paver layout rather than a straight strip, which is more a layout question than a product one.</p>"),
        ("Why a checkerboard patio uses one product across every square",
         "<p>A checkerboard patio alternating paver squares with turf squares reads as one design, so the turf inside every square on a {city} patio needs to be the same product, same pile height, same infill, rather than whatever was left over from other jobs. Mixing products across squares shows up quickly as some squares fading or wearing at a different rate than the rest, since even turf built to last ten to twenty years ages differently depending on stabilization and infill. Ordering enough of one product to cover every square from the start avoids a patchwork look setting in within the first few seasons.</p>"),
        ("Choosing coated sand for the strip that sits next to hot pavers",
         "<p>Concrete or clay pavers absorb and hold heat longer than open ground, and the turf blades right at a {city} paver joint often run warmer as a result, which is one reason coated silica sand, allowed under the state's rule as a natural-material infill with a non-toxic coating, gets favored for that specific strip over plain silica sand. The coating's cooling property does more work in a spot that's already running hotter than average than it would spread across an open lawn with no paver heat nearby. The rest of the yard can still use plain sand where that extra margin isn't needed.</p>"),
        ("Sizing a strip's backing for more than its own rainfall",
         "<p>An open lawn's backing only has to clear the rain that falls directly on it, but a {city} turf strip running beside a paver patio also receives whatever water sheets off the impervious paver surface next to it during a storm, since pavers themselves don't absorb rainfall the way soil does. A backing rated well above the standard drainage minimum accounts for that extra volume arriving from the side rather than just from above, which matters more on a strip bordered by a large paved area than on a turf section with only open yard around it.</p>"),
        ("When a strip is narrow enough to need the denser ribbon product",
         "<p>A turf ribbon only a few inches wide needs the shorter, denser blade a driveway strip typically gets, but a wider paver-bordered turf panel, several feet across inside a {city} patio's checkerboard layout, behaves more like an open lawn section and can use a standard residential-density product instead. The narrower the strip, the more a taller pile leans or mats without surrounding grass to hold it upright, so the width itself, not just the strip's role as a border, actually decides which density a given section calls for.</p>"),
    ],
    "process": [
        ("Laying out a ribbon before a single paver gets touched",
         "<p>The first step on a {city} paver-and-turf job is measuring the joint width against the existing paver pattern and snapping a chalk line, not cutting turf. Strip widths on a driveway ribbon or a checkerboard patio have to track the paver module exactly, since even a good hardscape job is rarely perfectly straight over its full length, and a layout that assumes it is ends up with a strip that narrows or widens visibly partway down. We walk the full run with a tape measure before any material is cut, catching those drifts while they're still easy to correct on paper.</p>"),
        ("Cutting turf tight to a paver's chamfered edge",
         "<p>Turf for a paver joint is cut a little wide first and trimmed down on site, rather than cut to the exact final width off a measurement taken earlier in the day. A {city} paver's chamfered top edge changes the effective joint width slightly compared with a square-edged paver, so the final trim happens against the actual edge in front of the installer, not against a number on a tape measure. Trimming tight this way also lets the crew correct for any small drift in the paver line without having to recut a whole strip from scratch.</p>"),
        ("Anchoring a ribbon into the base, not into the paver",
         "<p>Every nail or bead of adhesive on a {city} turf strip goes into the turf's own base material beside the paver, never into the paver itself, so the strip can be lifted later for a repair without disturbing the hardscape next to it. Edge anchoring gets checked twice on a ribbon or a stepping-stone path, since seams and edges here carry more stress per linear foot than they would on a wide-open lawn where there's more turf to absorb movement. A perimeter that would pass on a full lawn sometimes needs tighter nail spacing specifically because a narrow strip has so little slack to work with.</p>"),
        ("Compacting the whole footprint before it splits into two bases",
         "<p>We grade and compact the full paver-and-turf footprint as one continuous surface before drawing the line between the dense-graded paver zone and the washed, open-graded turf zone, so both materials share the same finished elevation from the start. Splitting the footprint into its two base types too early, before the overall grade is settled, is how a {city} project ends up with a paver course sitting slightly proud of or below the turf strip once everything is compacted in place. This sequencing step happens before either aggregate is ordered, since it decides how much of each material the job actually needs.</p>"),
        ("Brushing infill into a narrow strip by hand",
         "<p>Infill on an open lawn gets broadcast and worked in with a drop spreader, but a {city} ribbon only a few inches wide gets its infill brushed in by hand instead, since a spreader throws material past the strip and into the paver joints on either side. Working infill in against the grain with a stiff brush, a little at a time, keeps the rate close to the standard one to two pounds per square foot without overloading the joint sand next to it. This last step takes longer per square foot than an open lawn does, which is part of why a narrow strip prices differently than its size alone would suggest.</p>"),
        ("Setting edge restraint before turf ever gets anchored",
         "<p>Once the paver-and-turf footprint is graded and compacted as one surface on a {city} job, installing the edge restraint, whether a metal strip, bender board, or the paver's own edge course, happens before any turf nail or adhesive bead goes in along that joint. Anchoring turf into a joint that doesn't have its restraint set yet risks the edge shifting once the restraint does go in later, undoing work that was supposed to be finished. Sequencing the restraint ahead of the turf's own anchoring keeps that step from having to be redone.</p>"),
        ("Confirming the septic lid before pavers or turf get set nearby",
         "<p>Before bedding sand or base rock goes down for a {city} paver-and-turf walkway that crosses anywhere near a septic drainfield, the crew confirms the tank lid's exact location and flags it, the same way a buried irrigation head gets flagged ahead of excavation. This happens early in the process specifically because a paver-and-turf walkway is harder to partially lift and reset than open turf alone once both materials are fully bedded, so catching the lid's position before either material goes down avoids a much larger fix later.</p>"),
        ("Why a paver-and-turf job waits for a dry window, not just a dry day",
         "<p>Fresh paver joint sand and fresh turf infill both need time to settle without a downpour disturbing either one, so scheduling a {city} paver-and-turf combination job around the June-through-September rainy season means looking for a multi-day dry stretch rather than just a single rain-free morning. A storm hitting partway through can wash uncured joint sand into the adjacent turf strip or push turf infill into unfinished paver joints, mixing the two materials at the exact boundary that was supposed to stay clean between them.</p>"),
    ],
    "risks": [
        ("Why a ribbon beside sunbaked pavers runs hotter",
         "<p>Full summer sun already pushes turf surface temperature to 120 to 150 degrees, and concrete or clay pavers next to a {city} ribbon absorb and hold that heat longer than open ground does, so the blades along the paver edge often run a few degrees hotter than turf in the middle of an open lawn. A hose rinse still pulls the surface down 30 to 50 degrees within minutes, but on a paver-and-turf strip that relief doesn't last as long, since the paver keeps radiating stored heat back into the turf beside it after the water has drained through.</p>"),
        ("Narrow strips lift first when wind gets under an edge",
         "<p>A wide lawn has enough turf and infill weight spread across its footprint to resist wind getting underneath any one spot, but a narrow ribbon or stepping-stone path on a {city} property has far less material per linear foot of edge, so a gust that would barely move a full lawn can catch and lift a thin strip's corner. That's part of why edge anchoring gets extra attention on this kind of layout; a nail spacing that's adequate on an open yard can leave a narrow strip more exposed than the same standard would suggest.</p>"),
        ("Where infill actually goes during a heavy summer storm",
         "<p>Central Florida storms routinely drop one to three inches of rain in an hour, and on an open lawn that volume spreads across a wide area before it reaches an edge. A {city} turf strip pinned between two paver courses doesn't have that room, so infill worked loose by heavy rain has a shorter, more direct path into the paver joint next door instead of just settling deeper into the turf. A ribbon that loses infill this way looks thin along one edge well before the rest of the strip shows any wear at all.</p>"),
        ("Tire wear turns a driveway ribbon brown fast",
         "<p>Turf laid directly in a vehicle's wheel path takes a kind of damage a lawn never sees: repeated point-load crushing from tire weight, plus oil and brake dust that stain the blades in a way a hose rinse doesn't fully clear. A {city} driveway ribbon avoids this by running down the center or along the border, never in the tracks themselves, but a layout that misjudges where a typical car's wheels actually fall ends up with a strip that browns and mats within a season. Measuring against an actual vehicle's wheelbase, not just the driveway's overall width, is what keeps the ribbon out of the tire path.</p>"),
        ("Setbacks that shrink a checkerboard patio near the water",
         "<p>A checkerboard patio planned near a pond, canal, or conservation edge on a {city} lot still has to keep every turf square at least ten feet from the water unless a seawall or bulkhead already separates the two, and that setback can force the layout to swap several squares nearest the water back to paver or another hard surface instead of turf. Skipping this check until after the pattern is drawn means redrawing the whole checkerboard later, since the setback applies to the turf squares specifically and doesn't average out across a mixed-material patio.</p>"),
        ("What happens when edge restraint gets skipped to save a step",
         "<p>Skipping or undersizing the edge restraint at a {city} paver-and-turf joint doesn't cause an obvious problem on installation day, since both materials sit exactly where they were placed, but over a few seasons of foot traffic, rain, and normal ground movement, the dense-graded paver base and the washed-rock turf base can migrate into each other along that line. The clean joint that was cut and compacted at the start gradually blurs into an uneven boundary, and correcting it later means pulling up both materials at the joint rather than simply adding the restraint that should have gone in the first time.</p>"),
        ("A path laid over a septic lid nobody checked for first",
         "<p>Setting pavers or gluing turf across a {city} walkway route without confirming the septic tank's lid location first risks covering access that has to stay reachable, and a paver-and-turf path is harder to partially undo than an open lawn would be once both materials are bedded in place. Finding the buried lid after the fact usually means cutting back a finished section of walkway rather than adjusting a layout that was still just a chalk line, turning what should have been a five-minute check into a redo of finished work.</p>"),
        ("Assuming a mixed-material path near water is exempt because of the pavers",
         "<p>The state's ten-foot waterbody setback applies to turf specifically, not to pavers, so a {city} path or patio mixing both materials near a pond or canal can't assume the whole layout is exempt just because part of it is hardscape. A design that treats the pavers as covering the setback question for the entire footprint risks having its turf sections flagged during review, or built too close and pulled back later, since the rule tracks where the turf itself sits regardless of what material runs alongside it.</p>"),
    ],
    "care": [
        ("Why a paver-and-turf strip collects more grit than open lawn",
         "<p>A narrow ribbon or path on a {city} property tends to gather windblown grit and sand faster than an open lawn does, since the hard paver edges on either side channel debris along the strip instead of letting it settle randomly across a wider area. Rinsing the strip along with the rest of the yard during a regular hose pass usually clears it, but a paver-and-turf layout benefits from a slightly closer look each time, since a buildup that would spread out and go unnoticed on a lawn shows up as a visible line along a joint.</p>"),
        ("Brushing a ribbon back from the paver's shaded side",
         "<p>Blades along a {city} ribbon tend to lean toward whichever paver course throws more shade during the day, since less direct sun on that side means the pile springs back more slowly after being walked on. Brushing against the grain every few weeks corrects that lean before it sets in permanently, working from the shaded edge back toward the center of the strip rather than in one direction across the whole ribbon. Catching this early keeps a paver-and-turf job looking like one continuous strip instead of two different heights meeting at a line.</p>"),
        ("Checking infill depth in a strip that loses it faster",
         "<p>A narrow joint holds proportionally less infill than an open lawn to begin with, so the same rate of loss from rinsing and rain shows up as a visibly thin strip sooner on a {city} ribbon than it would across a full yard. We check infill depth in a paver-and-turf strip on a tighter interval than we would a lawn, since there's less material there to buffer a slow, steady loss before the backing starts to show through along the paver edge.</p>"),
        ("Walking the paver edge after a named storm",
         "<p>After heavy wind or rain, the paver-and-turf joint on a {city} property is worth checking specifically, since the anchor points along that edge carry more stress per linear foot than a full lawn's perimeter does. A nail that's started to pull loose near a paver course, or a corner that's lifted a fraction of an inch, is easier to re-secure the same week than after it's had another storm to work at it. This check takes only a few minutes along a ribbon or path, far less time than the same walk around an open lawn's full perimeter.</p>"),
        ("Two materials, two cleaning schedules on the same walkway",
         "<p>The turf and the paver joints on a {city} stepping-stone path or ribbon are different materials with different refresh needs, even though they sit inches apart. Turf infill gets topped up on the same schedule as any other turf project, while the sand between the pavers themselves needs its own separate refresh as it washes out or compacts over time. Treating both as one maintenance task usually means one of the two gets neglected, since a crew focused on brushing turf pile can walk right past paver joint sand that's already thinned to the point of letting the pavers shift.</p>"),
        ("Checking the edge restraint itself, not just the turf and pavers",
         "<p>A yearly look at a {city} paver-and-turf joint is worth extending to the restraint hardware itself, the metal strip, bender board, or paver edge course, not just the visible turf pile and the paver surface on either side of it. A restraint spike that's worked loose or an edging strip that's crept slightly out of line usually shows up as a subtle waviness along the joint before it becomes an obvious gap, and catching that early keeps the boundary crisp instead of needing a full joint rebuild once the restraint has fully failed.</p>"),
        ("Keeping a septic lid clear along a path that gets brushed regularly",
         "<p>Routine brushing and infill top-ups along a {city} paver-and-turf walkway can gradually work material toward a septic lid near the path, covering it a little more with each pass unless someone deliberately checks that spot separately. Keeping the lid visibly clear, rather than assuming it'll turn up when it's needed, saves a search or a partial excavation the day a pump-out truck actually arrives. This is a small addition to a normal care routine, worth a specific look rather than an assumption that general upkeep handles it.</p>"),
        ("Extra attention to a waterside patio once the wet season ends",
         "<p>A {city} paver-and-turf patio sitting near a pond or canal is worth a closer look right after the wet season than an equivalent patio farther from water, since runoff patterns near a waterbody can wash infill or joint sand toward the setback line in a way an interior-lot patio never experiences. Checking both the turf's infill depth and the paver joints' sand level along that waterside edge specifically, rather than assuming the whole patio wore evenly, catches a section that lost more material than the rest of the layout did over the same few months.</p>"),
    ],
}
